Hold $x$ constant. The numerator contains a composite function, so use the chain rule to differentiate it:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial}{\partial y}e^{xy^2}
&=e^{xy^2}\frac{\partial}{\partial y}(xy^2)=2xy e^{xy^2}, \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial y}(x+y^2)&=2y.
\end{aligned}
$$

Both numerator and denominator depend on $y$. Apply the quotient rule, preserving the subtraction and the squared denominator:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}
&=\frac{(2xy e^{xy^2})(x+y^2)-e^{xy^2}(2y)}{(x+y^2)^2} \\
&=\frac{2y e^{xy^2}\bigl(x(x+y^2)-1\bigr)}{(x+y^2)^2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The factor $2y e^{xy^2}$ is common to the numerator terms; it cannot be canceled with the sum $x+y^2$ in the denominator. The derivative is defined wherever $x+y^2\ne0$.
